// View surfaces: a surface in the world that shows the scene drawn again from another camera.
//
// A MIRROR and a PORTAL are the two kinds so far; a security-camera screen or a window onto another place would be the
// same piece with a different camera. The piece is three steps, and only the first differs between kinds:
//
//   1. WHICH CAMERA. 'cameraThrough' says, from the viewer's camera and the surface's place in the world, the
//      camera whose picture the surface shows. For a mirror it is the viewer's camera reflected about the
//      surface's plane (render's Camera.reflectedAbout: the eye reflected, nothing behind the plane drawn). For a
//      portal it is the viewer's camera moved rigidly by the surface's LINK (a translation: the portal's exit is the
//      surface's place plus the link), with the exit's plane as the near plane, so nothing between the moved camera
//      and the exit is drawn (Lengyel's oblique clip, as the mirror's). Nothing else changes.
//   2. DRAW THE SCENE AGAIN from that camera into the surface's own texture (render's RenderView, a view of
//      the same draw list on the layers the surface is not on, so a surface is left out of its own picture), lit
//      by the light of the world the picture shows (the island's sun, or the labyrinth's lamps alone).
//   3. SHOW THE PICTURE on the surface: the browser's own custom pipeline (surfaceview.slang) that reads the picture
//      at the fragment's own screen position.
//
// RENDER TO TEXTURE, NOT THE STENCIL. A portal could be drawn by stencil (write its opening, draw the far side where
// the stencil matches). The render package has no stencil in its passes, and its RenderView gives the same picture
// in a texture the mirror already uses; the cost is a texture a surface (which a stencil does not have), and the
// recursion depth is one by construction: a surface is on its own layer, which no picture draws, so a portal seen
// through a portal shows what is behind it, not another picture.
//
// THE WINDOW. A picture is not the frame's view but the part of it the surface covers: the surface's screen rectangle
// (a little over), from a camera whose frustum is cut to it (the field of view narrowed, the middle shifted by the
// camera's jitter), drawn into a texture just big enough to hold it at the frame's own sharpness times 'scale'. So the
// second pass culls, shadows and draws only what the surface can show, and its fill is the surface's own. The
// surface's material reads the picture at the window (surfaceview.slang's 'crop').
//
// A surface is a part whose look names SHADER_MIRROR (parts.cone): the look's params are its tint (rgb), its kind (the
// fourth number), the link (the next three: where its exit is, less where it is) and the world its picture shows (the
// last: DEST_DAY or DEST_UNDER); the part's mesh is mesh's plane (-1 to 1 in x and z, facing +y, so +y is the
// surface's normal), and where it is is the part's world matrix. So the simulation places it like any part
// (the cottage's wall carries the looking-glass), and the view only draws what it shows.
//
// The picture is drawn only for a surface that is in front of the viewer's eye (the plane's front side) and
// whose quad is in the viewer's frustum; a surface nobody sees costs nothing.
//
// The layer: the surface's own draw item is on layer 2 and the picture's view draws layer 1 only (render's
// RenderView.layerMask), so the surface is not in its own picture.

// (The kinds of surface, SURFACE_MIRROR, SURFACE_PORTAL and SURFACE_GLASS, and the worlds a picture shows, DEST_DAY and DEST_UNDER,
// are parts/portal.cone's, where the world that makes the looks can see them.)

// The layer bit a surface's item is on; everything else is on layer 1 (render's default)
pub const SURFACE_LAYER = 2u32;

// How far past the surface's screen rectangle the window reaches, in the frame's clip space (the temporal AA's jitter and the
// filter's reach)
const WINDOW_MARGIN: f32 = 0.03;
// The smallest and largest side of a picture, texels
const PICTURE_MIN: u32 = 32u32;
const PICTURE_MAX: u32 = 4096u32;
// How far past the window a picture may be bigger than wanted before it is made smaller again (so a surface seen from near
// and from far does not remake its picture at every step)
const PICTURE_SLACK: u32 = 4u32;
// A surface whose window is smaller than this on both sides (half sizes, frame pixels) is given no new picture: it shows the last it held. A pane
// a few pixels across cannot show what a picture holds, and the picture costs the same whatever the pane's size (the island's terrain and sea again)
const PICTURE_SKIP_PIXELS: f32 = 10.;

// One surface
pub struct SurfaceRow {
  pub kind: u32;
  pub tint: Color;
  pub link: Vec3;                // a portal's exit, less the surface's place
  pub dest: u32;                 // and the world its picture shows (DEST_...)
  pub view: RenderView;          // its picture
  pub shows: MaterialHandle;     // the material showing the picture
  pub model: Mat4;               // the world matrix of its item in this frame's draw list
  pub seen: bool = false;        // there is one
  pub drawn: u64 = 0u64;         // pictures drawn, ever
  pub window: Vec4 = new Vec4(0., 0., 1., 1.);   // the picture's window on the frame: middle x, y and half sizes, clip space
  pub fresh: bool = false;       // 'plan' has found a window for this frame
  pub placed: bool = false;      // its place is known (it has been in a draw list)
}

// One world a picture can show: how it is lit and exposed, whether the sun casts shadows in it, how far it is seen
pub struct PictureWorld {
  pub light: Light;
  pub exposure: f32;
  pub shadows: bool;
  pub far: f32;
  pub environment: f32;          // how strongly the sky lights the physically based parts
  pub layers: u32;               // the layers of the draw list it is made of (the island's, or the labyrinth's: portal.cone's LAB_LAYER)
  pub fog: FogSettings;          // the air it shows: the weather's froxel fog (enabled while there is some) for a picture of the island, none for the labyrinth's
}

// The worlds the frame's pictures can show: the viewer's own (a mirror), the island's day, and the labyrinth
pub struct PictureEnv {
  pub own: PictureWorld;
  pub day: PictureWorld;
  pub under: PictureWorld;
}

// The surfaces of the world, by their look's material
pub struct ViewSurfaces {
  pipeline: PipelineHandle;
  ready: bool;
  pub rows: Table[SurfaceRow];
  pub open: bool = false;        // the looking-glass has opened: a SURFACE_GLASS is a portal
  pub pictures: u32 = 0u32;      // pictures drawn at the last frame
  pub ticks: u64 = 0u64;         // the CPU time recording them (performance counter ticks), over the frames that drew any
  pub frames: u64 = 0u64;        // and how many such frames
  pub width: u32 = 16u32;        // the frame's size, set by the render actor before it applies requests
  pub height: u32 = 16u32;
  // PEGASUS_MIRROR_TIMING=1: each picture is timed on the GPU (timestamps round its pass), the device waited idle after it, which
  // slows the frame: for measuring a picture's cost, not for playing
  pub timing: bool = false;
  pub gpuNs: f64 = 0.0;           // the GPU time of the pictures timed
  pub gpuPictures: u64 = 0u64;
  pub lodBias: f32 = 1.;
  pub scale: f32 = 1.;           // a picture's sharpness against the frame's (PEGASUS_MIRROR_SCALE, percent)
  pub windowed: bool = true;     // pictures cut to the surface's window (PEGASUS_MIRROR_WINDOW=0: the whole frame's view, for measuring)
  pub shadowDistance: f32 = 0.;  // how far the sun's shadows reach in a picture, metres (PEGASUS_MIRROR_SHADOWD; 0: as the frame's, 120). A nearer limit
                                // (50) saved about 1.6 ms of CPU a frame but lost the cottage's shadow on the lawn in the reflection (mirror-eyes) for a reason
                                // not found, so it is not the default; an integrated GPU takes 30 (see 'tier')
  pub lastCalls: u32 = 0u32;     // the draws and triangles of the last picture
  pub lastTriangles: u64 = 0u64;
  pub lastTexels: u64 = 0u64;    // the texels of the pictures drawn at the last frame
  pub dayWanted: bool = false;   // a picture of the island is wanted this frame (a surface that shows it is seen: the draw list must hold the island)
  pub labWanted: bool = false;   // and one of the labyrinth (the glass is open and seen): else, on the island, the labyrinth's items are left out of the list
  pub cascades: u32 = 0u32;      // how many cascades of the sun's shadows a picture draws (PEGASUS_MIRROR_CASCADES, 1 to 4; 0: the frame's)
  pub off: bool = false;         // PEGASUS_NO_PICTURES=1: no picture is drawn (a surface shows what it last held): to see what the pictures cost the frame
  envScale: bool = false;        // the environment said the picture's sharpness: not the tier's to set
  pub fogAllowed: bool = true;   // PEGASUS_NO_PICTURE_FOG=1: the pictures are left unfogged, to see what their fog costs
  pub fogOn: bool = false;       // the pictures carry the froxel fog (the frame has image quality on: renderactor's 'fxTier' above 0)
  pub fogBase: FogSettings = new FogSettings();   // the grid and reach a picture's fog is made for (the tier's): a picture keeps its grid, or its volume is remade

  // The pictures by the GPU's tier: on an integrated GPU (fill-bound) sharper than 0.6 of the frame's is not worth the fill; unless the
  // environment said otherwise
  pub fn tier(self: &mut, integrated: bool) {
    if !integrated { return; }
    if !envScale { scale = 0.6; }
  }

  pub fn make() ViewSurfaces {
    var s = new ViewSurfaces(PipelineHandle.none(), false, Table[SurfaceRow].empty());
    let e = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_MIRROR_TIMING");
    if (e as usize) != 0usize { s.timing = libc.strcmp(cstr.fromPtr(e), "1") == 0i32; }
    let l = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_MIRROR_LOD");
    if (l as usize) != 0usize { s.lodBias = f32.from(u32.from(wholeNumber(l))) / 100.; }
    let sc = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_MIRROR_SCALE");
    if (sc as usize) != 0usize { s.scale = f32.from(u32.from(wholeNumber(sc))) / 100.; s.envScale = true; }
    let sd = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_MIRROR_SHADOWD");
    if (sd as usize) != 0usize { s.shadowDistance = f32.from(u32.from(wholeNumber(sd))); }
    let cs = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_MIRROR_CASCADES");
    if (cs as usize) != 0usize { s.cascades = u32.from(wholeNumber(cs)); }
    let np = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_NO_PICTURES");
    if (np as usize) != 0usize { s.off = libc.strcmp(cstr.fromPtr(np), "1") == 0i32; }
    let nf = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_NO_PICTURE_FOG");
    if (nf as usize) != 0usize { s.fogAllowed = libc.strcmp(cstr.fromPtr(nf), "1") != 0i32; }
    let wn = libc.getenv("PEGASUS_MIRROR_WINDOW");
    if (wn as usize) != 0usize { s.windowed = libc.strcmp(cstr.fromPtr(wn), "0") != 0i32; }
    s
  }

  // The material of a surface showing 'picture', tinted 'tint', for the mirror (flip 1) or the portal (0)
  fn materialFor(self: &mut, renderer: &mut Renderer, device: &mut Device, picture: TextureHandle, tint: Color, window: Vec4, flip: f32) MaterialHandle {
    let params = [tint.red, tint.green, tint.blue, 1.,  window.x, window.y, 1. / window.z, 1. / window.w,  flip, 0., 0., 0.,  0., 0., 0., 0.];
    let maps = [picture];
    renderer.addCustomMaterial(device, pipeline, &params, &maps)
  }

  fn makeView(self: &, renderer: &mut Renderer, device: &mut Device, w: u32, h: u32) RenderView {
    var view = RenderView.create(renderer, device, w, h);
    view.layerMask = 1u32;
    view.lodBias = lodBias;
    if timing { view.enableTiming(device); }
    // The froxel fog of the frame, over the picture (render's RenderView.enableFog, Cone #613): the picture's own volume, the size of
    // the picture; drawn while 'fog.enabled', which 'drawSurfaceViews' sets from the world the picture shows. (It stalls the device once.)
    if fogOn {
      view.fog = *(&self.fogBase);
      view.fog.enabled = false;
      view.enableFog(renderer, device);
    }
    view
  }

  // Material 'id' is a surface of 'kind', tinted 'tint', its exit 'link' away, showing world 'dest': its picture made small
  // now and sized by 'plan' when it is seen. The material that shows it.
  pub fn define(self: &mut, renderer: &mut Renderer, device: &mut Device, id: MaterialId, kind: u32, tint: Color, link: Vec3, dest: u32) MaterialHandle {
    if !ready {
      let textures = [TEXTURE_SAMPLE_TYPE_FLOAT];
      let desc = new CustomPipeline(spirv: &SURFACE_SPIRV, fragmentEntryPoint: "surfaceFragment", textures: &textures,
        streams: 1u32, paramBytes: 64u64);
      pipeline = renderer.addPipeline(device, &desc);
      ready = true;
    }
    let view = self.makeView(renderer, device, PICTURE_MIN, PICTURE_MIN);
    let shows = self.materialFor(renderer, device, view.color, tint, new Vec4(0., 0., 1., 1.), if kind == SURFACE_PORTAL { 0. } else { 1. });
    rows.put(id.id, new SurfaceRow(kind, tint, link, dest, view, shows, Mat4.identity()));
    shows
  }

  pub fn isSurface(self: &, id: MaterialId) bool { rows.find(id.id) != NO_ROW }

  // Item of surface 'id' is in this frame's draw list, placed by 'model'
  pub fn note(self: &mut, id: MaterialId, model: Mat4) {
    let r = rows.find(id.id);
    if r == NO_ROW { return; }
    let row = &mut rows.rows[r];
    row.seen = true;
    row.placed = true;
    row.model = model;
  }

  // The material showing surface number 'i''s picture
  pub fn materialOf(self: &, i: usize) MaterialHandle { rows.rows[i].shows }

  pub fn anySeen(self: &) bool {
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < rows.len() {
      if rows.rows[i].seen { return true; }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    false
  }

  // The size of a picture wanted for a window of half sizes 'hx' and 'hy' (clip space) on a frame 'width' by 'height': a power of
  // two a side, so it is remade seldom
  fn wanted(self: &, half: f32, frame: u32) u32 {
    let texels = half * f32.from(frame) * scale;
    var n = PICTURE_MIN;
    while f32.from(n) < texels && n < PICTURE_MAX { n = n * 2u32; }
    n
  }

  // Before the draw list is built, once a frame: for each surface seen last frame (its place is known) that the viewer faces
  // and sees, its window on the frame, and its picture made the size that holds it. True if any picture was remade (its
  // material is then another: see 'materialOf').
  pub fn plan(self: &mut, renderer: &mut Renderer, device: &mut Device, camera: &Camera, width: u32, height: u32) bool {
    var changed = false;
    let eye = camera.position();
    let aspect = f32.from(width) / f32.from(height);
    let viewProjection = camera.projectionMatrix(aspect) * camera.viewMatrix();
    dayWanted = false;
    labWanted = false;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < rows.len() {
      let row = &mut rows.rows[i];
      row.fresh = false;
      if row.placed && facesEye(&row.model, eye) && inFrustum(&viewProjection, &row.model) {
        var window = new Vec4(0., 0., 1., 1.);
        if windowed { window = windowOf(&viewProjection, &row.model); }
        if windowed && window.z * f32.from(width) < PICTURE_SKIP_PIXELS && window.w * f32.from(height) < PICTURE_SKIP_PIXELS {
          i = i + 1usize;
          continue;
        }
        // The window's half sizes, grown to the texture's: a texel is a frame pixel times 'scale', whatever the size
        let w = self.wanted(window.z, width);
        let h = self.wanted(window.w, height);
        let vw = row.view.width;
        let vh = row.view.height;
        var tw = vw;
        var th = vh;
        if w > vw || w * PICTURE_SLACK <= vw { tw = w; }
        if h > vh || h * PICTURE_SLACK <= vh { th = h; }
        if tw != vw || th != vh {
          // (the picture being let go may be in use by the frame before)
          device.waitIdle();
          // (the old picture and the material that showed it are given back to render, which destroys them once the GPU is done)
          renderer.removeMaterial(device, row.shows);
          row.view.release(renderer, device);
          row.view = self.makeView(renderer, device, tw, th);
          row.shows = self.materialFor(renderer, device, row.view.color, row.tint, window, 0.);   // (its params are written at each draw)
          changed = true;
        }
        row.window = new Vec4(window.x, window.y, f32.from(tw) / (f32.from(width) * scale), f32.from(th) / (f32.from(height) * scale));
        row.fresh = true;
        if portalNow(row.kind, open) && row.dest == DEST_DAY { dayWanted = true; }
        if portalNow(row.kind, open) && row.dest == DEST_UNDER { labWanted = true; }
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    changed
  }
}

// (physics.cone's 'atLeast' is used here too)

// Whether a surface of 'kind' shows through a portal now (a glass that has opened, or a portal), not in a mirror
fn portalNow(kind: u32, open: bool) bool { kind == SURFACE_PORTAL || (kind == SURFACE_GLASS && open) }

// ---- Step 1: which camera ----

// The plane of a surface placed by 'model': through its place, facing its +y (the plane mesh's front), turned
// to face 'eye': (n, d) with n.p + d = 0 on the plane and the eye on the positive side
fn surfacePlane(model: &Mat4, eye: Vec3) Vec4 {
  let point = new Vec3(model.m[12], model.m[13], model.m[14]);
  let normal = new Vec3(model.m[4], model.m[5], model.m[6]);
  mirrorPlane(point, normal, eye)
}

// Whether the surface placed by 'model' faces 'eye' (its back is never seen: the item is culled)
fn facesEye(model: &Mat4, eye: Vec3) bool {
  let point = new Vec3(model.m[12], model.m[13], model.m[14]);
  let normal = new Vec3(model.m[4], model.m[5], model.m[6]).normalize();
  normal.dot(eye - point) > 0.
}

// The camera whose picture a mirror placed by 'model' shows, for a viewer seeing it through 'camera'
pub fn cameraThrough(model: &Mat4, kind: u32, camera: &Camera) Camera {
  camera.reflectedAbout(surfacePlane(model, camera.position()))
}

// The camera whose picture a portal placed by 'model' shows, its exit 'link' away: the viewer's camera moved by the link
// (a rigid move, a translation), the exit's plane its near plane. The exit plane faces the way the viewer looks through
// the portal: the surface's, turned the other way, through the exit's place.
pub fn cameraThroughPortal(model: &Mat4, link: Vec3, camera: &Camera) Camera {
  let plane = surfacePlane(model, camera.position());
  let point = new Vec3(model.m[12], model.m[13], model.m[14]) + link;
  let n = new Vec3(plane.x, plane.y, plane.z);
  var c = *camera;
  c.origin = camera.origin + link;
  // (the exit plane replaces the near plane (Lengyel's oblique clip), which must be nearer than it: an eye about to go through the pane has
  // the exit plane as near as the camera's own near plane, where the oblique projection is degenerate, and the picture would be empty)
  c.near = 0.01;
  c.clipped = true;
  c.clip = new Vec4(0. - n.x, 0. - n.y, 0. - n.z, n.dot(point));
  c
}

// ---- Seen or not ----

fn clipOf(m: &Mat4, p: Vec3) Vec4 {
  new Vec4(m.m[0] * p.x + m.m[4] * p.y + m.m[8] * p.z + m.m[12], m.m[1] * p.x + m.m[5] * p.y + m.m[9] * p.z + m.m[13],
    m.m[2] * p.x + m.m[6] * p.y + m.m[10] * p.z + m.m[14], m.m[3] * p.x + m.m[7] * p.y + m.m[11] * p.z + m.m[15])
}

// Whether any part of the surface's quad (-1 to 1 in x and z of its frame) is inside the frustum of the
// viewer matrix 'viewProjection' (conservative: false only if all four corners lie past one plane)
fn inFrustum(viewProjection: &Mat4, model: &Mat4) bool {
  var all = 0xffu32;
  var c = 0u32;
  while c < 4u32 {
    let sx = if (c & 1u32) == 0u32 { -1.f } else { 1. };
    let sz = if (c & 2u32) == 0u32 { -1.f } else { 1. };
    let world = clipOf(model, new Vec3(sx, 0., sz));
    let p = clipOf(viewProjection, new Vec3(world.x, world.y, world.z));
    var code = 0u32;
    if p.x < 0. - p.w { code = code | 1u32; }
    if p.x > p.w { code = code | 2u32; }
    if p.y < 0. - p.w { code = code | 4u32; }
    if p.y > p.w { code = code | 8u32; }
    // (behind the eye's own plane, not nearer than the near plane: a surface nearer than it is not cut away by it, surfaceview.slang)
    if p.z < 0. && p.w <= 0.0001 { code = code | 16u32; }
    if p.z > p.w { code = code | 32u32; }
    all = all & code;
    c = c + 1u32;
  }
  all == 0u32
}

// The window of the surface placed by 'model' on the frame of 'viewProjection': the rectangle of its four corners (and a
// margin) in clip space, as its middle and half sizes; the whole frame if a corner is behind the eye or too near it
fn windowOf(viewProjection: &Mat4, model: &Mat4) Vec4 {
  var x0 = 1000.f;
  var x1 = -1000.f;
  var y0 = 1000.f;
  var y1 = -1000.f;
  var c = 0u32;
  while c < 4u32 {
    let sx = if (c & 1u32) == 0u32 { -1.f } else { 1. };
    let sz = if (c & 2u32) == 0u32 { -1.f } else { 1. };
    let world = clipOf(model, new Vec3(sx, 0., sz));
    let p = clipOf(viewProjection, new Vec3(world.x, world.y, world.z));
    if p.w < 0.05 { return new Vec4(0., 0., 1., 1.); }
    let nx = p.x / p.w;
    let ny = p.y / p.w;
    if nx < x0 { x0 = nx; }
    if nx > x1 { x1 = nx; }
    if ny < y0 { y0 = ny; }
    if ny > y1 { y1 = ny; }
    c = c + 1u32;
  }
  x0 = atLeast(x0 - WINDOW_MARGIN, -1.);
  y0 = atLeast(y0 - WINDOW_MARGIN, -1.);
  x1 = 1. - atLeast(1. - (x1 + WINDOW_MARGIN), 0.);
  y1 = 1. - atLeast(1. - (y1 + WINDOW_MARGIN), 0.);
  let hx = atLeast((x1 - x0) * 0.5, 0.01);
  let hy = atLeast((y1 - y0) * 0.5, 0.01);
  new Vec4((x0 + x1) * 0.5, (y0 + y1) * 0.5, hx, hy)
}

// The camera of a picture cut to the window 'win' of the frame (middle x, y and half sizes, clip space; the frame's field of view
// 'fov'): the field of view narrowed to the window's height, its middle brought to the middle of the picture by the camera's jitter,
// which also carries the temporal AA's shift in the picture's own units: 'jitterX' and 'jitterY' are the frame's (clip space), the
// picture shifted the opposite way in x if it is a mirror's (mirrored left to right) and the same way if it is a portal's. The
// picture's own aspect is the window's (its texture's size is made to be); surfaceview.slang reads it back.
pub fn fitWindow(other: &mut Camera, fov: f32, win: Vec4, portal: bool, jitterX: f32, jitterY: f32) {
  let half = tan(fov * 0.5);
  other.fov = 2. * libc.atan2f(win.w * half, 1.);
  let sx = if portal { 1.f } else { -1. };
  other.jitterX = (sx * jitterX - (if portal { win.x } else { 0. - win.x })) / win.z;
  other.jitterY = (jitterY - win.y) / win.w;
}

// ---- Step 2: draw the scene again ----

// Draw the picture of every surface seen this frame, from the camera 'cameraFor' gives, of 'list' (the frame's
// draw list, the sky and birds in it) lit as 'env' says. Before the frame's own pass. How many pictures were drawn.
// The particles (the burner's flame, the chimney's smoke, the rain) are drawn into each picture after it, through the picture's
// own camera, over the picture's colour and against its depth (RenderView.texture lends the colour; render's pass does not draw
// particles). 'effects' says they were aimed this frame (the island's, not the labyrinth's): a picture of the labyrinth
// has none.
//
// 'jitterX' and 'jitterY' are the sub-pixel shift (clip space) that temporal AA is about to move the frame by. The pane reads the
// picture at the pixel it is drawn at, and that pixel is shifted by the frame's jitter, so for the pane's samples to be of
// the sequence the history averages, the picture is shifted the opposite way in x (a mirror's picture is mirrored left to right)
// or the same way (a portal's), and the same way in y.
pub fn drawSurfaceViews(surfaces: &mut ViewSurfaces, renderer: &mut Renderer, device: &mut Device, camera: &Camera,
    env: &PictureEnv, list: &DrawList, particles: &mut ParticleRenderer, effects: bool, cut: bool, jitterX: f32, jitterY: f32, width: u32, height: u32) u32 {
  var drawn = 0u32;
  var texels = 0u64;
  if surfaces.off { return 0u32; }
